Repair read.dcf() input lines with utf8Valid()'s escaping rules, and
double the field capacity instead of growing it by one.

do_readDCF() (src/main/dcf.c) repairs each line that is not valid
UTF-8 before matching it against the field names seen so far, so that
a name recorded with "<xx>" escapes matches its later occurrences.
The repair went through iconv() (reEnc3), which passes the withdrawn
5- and 6-byte UTF-8 forms through unchanged, while the name itself is
recorded with utf8Valid()'s rules (mkCharUTF8sub).  A field whose name
holds such a form therefore never matched, and every occurrence added
a column.  Repair the line with the same escaping as the name, split
out of mkCharUTF8sub() as escapeInvalidUTF8().

Each new field also reallocated the whole result matrix one row larger
and copied it element by element, with nrows()/ncols() recomputed for
every element, so discovering n fields cost O(n^2 * records): 7000
distinct fields in a 70 KB record took 7 s in a release build and
92 s under ASAN.  The row capacity now doubles, with nwhat rows (and
entries of 'what') in use, and the final transpose drops the spare
rows and trims 'what'.

Found by OSS-Fuzz (libFuzzer_r_dcf): testcase 5614980041605120, a
one-time Timeout in do_readDCF() on a 55 KB input whose field names
start with "\xfd\xb2\xb2\xb3\xb2\xb3" (a well-formed 6-byte form); 16
distinct fields became 2342 columns.  14 s under ASAN locally, 0.8 s
with this patch.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #451, filed 2026-11-03 (no Bugzilla report).  The
upstream PR also adds a regression test to tests/reg-tests-1e.R, left
out here so this carry patch does not go stale with that file.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk.

